 of what they're doing  now as and when the finger really does  point to Russia as I suspect it will in  in due course  um but the real response comes not  really from the EU but from NATO I mean  this will become a military issue in the  sense that NATO will have to look after  the security of the Baltic much more  carefully and they've got plans to do  that I mean in Britain we've been  talking about the um the vulnerability  of pipelines for a long time now for  some years and for undersea cables  because most of the undersea cables that  come across the Atlantic come into  Land's End and they go through the uh  through the area of the channel and the  Western approaches and so we've started  to deploy Naval forces to look after  those cables because we know that  they're vulnerable and so this this  um the battle to defend what's on the  seabed is been something that's been  developing over recent years and it's  now become I think or will become a  reality in the Baltic in the coming one  or two years what about what's on top of  the series speaking to the Lord Janet  yesterday and he was suggesting that he  wouldn't be resting comfortably he was  part of the Black Sea Fleet at the  moment  um no the Black Sea Fleet is now under  some pressure they lost the mosqueva  which was the flagship of course which  was attacked by the ukrainians and they  haven't been able to add to that because  they can't bring any more warships from  their Northern Fleet through the through  the Bosphorus because they're not  allowed to Turkey won't let them good  for them and so the Black Sea Fleet is  baked in sevastopol savastapol in Crimea  is now vulnerable to Ukrainian attacks  and the whole of the backs of most of  the Black Sea fleet has now been  deployed further eastwards in the Black  Sea so the western part of the Black Sea  around Odessa has become something of a  no-go area for the the Russian Black Sea  Fleet and you can see that pressure  building up again everywhere 